/**
@page status_architecture Status Module - Architecture

@section status_architecture_overview Design Overview

The Status module defines a unified error handling system across the entire
framework. Every operation returns a `rte_status_t` code that indicates
success or the specific failure reason.

**Design principle:** Consistency. All modules use the same status codes,
making error handling predictable and consistent.

@section status_architecture_status_codes Status Code Definitions

The numeric values are part of the ABI (`rte_status.h` is authoritative): existing entries are never renumbered,
new ones are appended.

```c
typedef enum {
    RTE_STATUS_OK                  = 0,  // Success
    RTE_STATUS_INVALID_PARAM       = 1,  // Null pointer or out-of-range argument
    RTE_STATUS_NOT_INITIALIZED     = 2,  // Object used before create/init
    RTE_STATUS_ALREADY_INITIALIZED = 3,  // Object already created/initialized
    RTE_STATUS_TIMEOUT             = 4,  // Blocking call exceeded its deadline
    RTE_STATUS_RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED  = 5,  // Static pool/storage/slots full
    RTE_STATUS_NOT_SUPPORTED       = 6,  // Valid request, OSAdapter cannot perform it
    RTE_STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED     = 7,  // OSAdapter is a stub
    RTE_STATUS_HARDWARE_FAULT      = 8,  // OSAdapter reported a hardware-level fault
    RTE_STATUS_DATA_CORRUPTION     = 9,  // Integrity check (e.g. NVM CRC) failed
    RTE_STATUS_INTERNAL_ERROR      = 10, // Defensive catch-all
    RTE_STATUS_VALUE_OUT_OF_RANGE  = 11, // Checked cast: value does not fit
    RTE_STATUS_INVALID_STATE       = 12, // Not permitted in the current lifecycle phase
    RTE_STATUS_NOT_CONNECTED       = 13  // Listen-role link has no peer yet; nothing sent (REQ-CHANSVC-001)
} rte_status_t;
```

@section status_architecture_code_allocation Code Allocation Strategy

```
0: RTE_STATUS_OK (success - no error)
1-13: Framework core status codes (parameter, timeout, hardware, etc.)
14-999: Reserved for framework expansion
1000+: Application-specific error codes (user can extend)
```

@section status_architecture_rationale Design Rationale

### Why Unified Status Codes?

**Without framework status codes:**
```c
int timer_result = rte_timer_create(...);  // What does -1 mean?
bool buffer_result = rte_buffer_write(...); // What does false mean?
errno = ...; // Different modules use different mechanisms
```
Problem: Inconsistent error handling across modules.

**With framework status codes:**
```c
rte_status_t timer_rc = rte_timer_create(...);
rte_status_t buffer_rc = rte_buffer_write(...);
// Both return the SAME code type
// Same error handling everywhere
```

### Why Not Use errno?

- errno is thread-unsafe (global state)
- errno is platform-specific (POSIX assumption)
- errno values vary by system
- MISRA-compliant systems avoid global state

@section status_architecture_extension Application-Specific Codes

Applications can define their own status codes:

```c
// In application header
typedef enum {
    RTE_STATUS_OK = 0,
    // ... framework codes ...
    RTE_STATUS_NOT_SUPPORTED = 6,

    // Application-specific (starting from 1000)
    APP_STATUS_TRAIN_SPEED_LIMIT_EXCEEDED = 1000,
    APP_STATUS_TRACK_INTEGRITY_CHECK_FAILED = 1001,
    APP_STATUS_SIGNAL_ASPECT_UNKNOWN = 1002,
} app_status_t;
```

@section status_architecture_conversion String Conversion

The module provides `rte_status_to_string()` for diagnostics:

```c
rte_status_t rc = some_operation();
if (rc != RTE_STATUS_OK) {
    printf("Error: %s\n", rte_status_to_string(rc));
    // Output: "Error: TIMEOUT"
}
```

The strings are the short names without the `RTE_STATUS_` prefix (`"OK"`, `"TIMEOUT"`, `"NOT_CONNECTED"`, ...); a
value outside the enum yields `"UNKNOWN_STATUS"`. The result is never NULL.

**Implementation** (`src/utils/status/rte_status.c`, abridged):
```c
const char *rte_status_to_string(rte_status_t status)
{
    switch (status)
    {
        case RTE_STATUS_OK:                  return "OK";
        case RTE_STATUS_INVALID_PARAM:       return "INVALID_PARAM";
        case RTE_STATUS_TIMEOUT:             return "TIMEOUT";
        // ... one case per enumerator, up to RTE_STATUS_NOT_CONNECTED ...
        default:                             return "UNKNOWN_STATUS";
    }
}
```

@section status_architecture_usage_patterns Usage Patterns in Framework

### Pattern 1: Initialization Check

Every module follows this pattern:
```c
rte_status_t rte_module_init(config_t *config) {
    // Validate input
    if (config == NULL) {
        return RTE_STATUS_INVALID_PARAM;
    }

    // Check if already initialized
    if (is_initialized) {
        return RTE_STATUS_ALREADY_INITIALIZED;
    }

    // Perform initialization
    // ...

    return RTE_STATUS_OK;
}
```

### Pattern 2: Operation with Timeout

Operations that can timeout:
```c
rte_status_t rte_channel_recv(handle, buffer, size, timeout) {
    // Try to receive data within timeout
    // ...
    if (no_data_within_timeout) {
        return RTE_STATUS_TIMEOUT;  // Caller knows why
    }
    // ...
    return RTE_STATUS_OK;
}
```

### Pattern 3: Resource Exhaustion

Operations that can fail due to capacity:
```c
rte_status_t rte_buffer_write(handle, data, size, written) {
    // Check buffer capacity
    if (buffer_remaining < size) {
        return RTE_STATUS_RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED;
    }
    // ...
    return RTE_STATUS_OK;
}
```

@section status_architecture_safestate Integration with Safe-State

Status codes inform safe-state decisions. The two snippets below are example application policy, not a rule the
framework enforces: which status from which call leads to a safe state is decided by the application (the framework
itself enters a safe state only where a requirement says so, e.g. REQ-CHECKPOINT-003, REQ-VOTER-005).

**Critical failure (example policy):**
```c
rte_status_t rc = vital_operation();
if (rc != RTE_STATUS_OK) {
    // Example policy: this application treats any failure of this vital call as unsafe.
    // APP_REASON_VITAL_OP_FAILED: an application-defined reason code
    // (>= RTE_SAFESTATE_REASON_APPLICATION_BASE, see rte_safestate.h).
    RTE_SAFESTATE(RTE_SAFESTATE_LEVEL_SAFE, APP_REASON_VITAL_OP_FAILED);
}
```

**Non-critical operation:**
```c
rte_status_t rc = diagnostics_operation();
if (rc != RTE_STATUS_OK) {
    // Log but DON'T trigger safe-state
    log_debug("Diagnostics error: %s", rte_status_to_string(rc));
}
```

@section status_architecture_misra MISRA Compliance

The status module supports MISRA C:2012:

- ✓ Fixed return type (rte_status_t enum)
- ✓ No implicit conversions (explicit enum values)
- ✓ No global state (errno-free)
- ✓ Deterministic (same code always means same error)
- ✓ Thread-safe (no shared state)

@section status_architecture_implementation Implementation Details

### Files

- `include/rte/utils/status/rte_status.h` — Public API (the enum is authoritative)
- `src/utils/status/rte_status.c` — `rte_status_to_string()`

### String Conversion

`rte_status_to_string()` is a `switch` with one `case` per enumerator and a `default` clause (MISRA C:2012 rule
16.4) returning `"UNKNOWN_STATUS"`; there is no lookup table (see the Implementation snippet above).

Pros: no array indexed by an external value (no out-of-bounds read for a value outside the enum), and a value
outside the enum is handled explicitly by `default`.
Cons: a new enumerator needs its own `case` and its own entry in the unit test's `codes[]` list; with both, the
test catches a missing `case` (it would return `"UNKNOWN_STATUS"`).

@section status_architecture_testing Testing Strategy

### Unit Tests

`tests/status/test_rte_status.c` (abridged):

```c
rte_status_t codes[] = { RTE_STATUS_OK, ..., RTE_STATUS_NOT_CONNECTED };
for (i = 0; i < sizeof(codes) / sizeof(codes[0]); ++i) {
    const char *s = rte_status_to_string(codes[i]);
    assert(s != NULL);
    assert(strcmp(s, "UNKNOWN_STATUS") != 0);   // every known code has its own name
    // ... and no two codes share a value or a string
}
assert(strcmp(rte_status_to_string(RTE_STATUS_NOT_CONNECTED), "NOT_CONNECTED") == 0);

// Value outside the enum: the default clause
assert(strcmp(rte_status_to_string((rte_status_t)999), "UNKNOWN_STATUS") == 0);
```

@section status_architecture_future Future Extensions

Possible extensions (reserved code space):
- Additional timeout types (READ_TIMEOUT vs WRITE_TIMEOUT)
- Buffer-specific codes (BUFFER_EMPTY, BUFFER_FULL)
- Network codes (CONNECTION_REFUSED, CONNECTION_RESET)
- All would fit within 1000+ application-specific range
*/
